    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Shopify better than WooCommerce for beginners?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Shopify is better for beginners. It is a fully hosted platform, which means you do not need to install software, configure a server, or manage SSL certificates. You sign up, pick a theme, add products, and your store is live. WooCommerce requires installing WordPress, configuring hosting, setting up security plugins, and maintaining updates. For a first-time store owner without technical skills, Shopify gets you to launch faster and with fewer headaches.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Shopify or WooCommerce?</h3>
    <p>WooCommerce is cheaper to start because the plugin is free. However, you still pay for hosting, a domain, a theme, and premium plugins, which typically costs $10 to $50 per month for a small store. Shopify starts at $29 per month and includes hosting, SSL, and security. Once your store grows past a few hundred products and needs premium WooCommerce extensions, total costs often match or exceed Shopify. For a simple store, WooCommerce is cheaper. For a growing business, Shopify is more predictable.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from WooCommerce to Shopify?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from WooCommerce to Shopify. Shopify offers a free Store Importer app that transfers products, customers, and order history directly from WooCommerce. The process typically takes a few hours for stores under 1,000 products. You will need to manually redirect old URLs to preserve SEO, and some custom WooCommerce fields may not map cleanly to Shopify. Many merchants hire a migration specialist for stores with complex data or custom functionality.</p>

    <h3>Which platform has better SEO, Shopify or WooCommerce?</h3>
    <p>WooCommerce has slightly better SEO out of the box because it runs on WordPress, which gives you full control over URL structure, meta tags, and content hierarchy with plugins like Yoast SEO or Rank Math. Shopify SEO is solid but more rigid. It forces certain URL structures and does not allow deep URL customization. For most stores, the SEO difference is small and content quality matters more than platform choice. If SEO is your primary growth channel and you have technical skills, WooCommerce offers more control.</p>

    <h3>Does Shopify or WooCommerce handle high traffic better?</h3>
    <p>Shopify handles high traffic better out of the box because it is a fully managed cloud platform with unlimited bandwidth and automatic scaling during traffic spikes. WooCommerce runs on your own hosting, so performance depends on your hosting plan and server configuration. A well-optimized WooCommerce store on premium managed hosting can match Shopify, but it requires ongoing maintenance. For flash sales, viral campaigns, or Black Friday traffic, Shopify is the safer choice because you never have to worry about your server going down.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Shopify and WooCommerce together?</h3>
    <p>It is not practical to run both platforms for the same store, but some businesses use Shopify for the main store and WooCommerce for a secondary catalog or a separate brand. A more common approach is to start on Shopify for speed and simplicity, then evaluate whether WooCommerce offers enough flexibility to justify a migration as your needs grow. Most merchants pick one platform and stick with it, because maintaining two ecommerce systems doubles your operational overhead.</p>

